    "case_id": "745",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old female with a history of diabetes mellitus was hospitalized in December 2017. She had been diagnosed with mental illness in 1998 and was stable on quetiapine for many years. In 2016, she developed new symptoms including stupor. In November 2017, her condition deteriorated - she lay in bed with open eyes but would not speak or move, and developed abdominal distension. She would not swallow food or medicine.\n\nPhysical exam showed:- Abdominal distension and tympany- Absent bowel sounds - No guarding or rigidity of abdomen- Bilateral rigidity on neurological exam- Mental status: Patient laid still, eyes open but vacant stare, no eye contact- Well-groomed but mute with systemic stiffness- Maintained unusual postures when limbs manipulated- Bush-Francis Catatonia Rating Scale score: 23\n\n- Laboratory tests: Normal except for hypomagnesemia and hypokalemia- Imaging:  - Abdominal CT: Showed accumulated air and fecal matter, dilated intestine with air-fluid levels but no mechanical obstruction  - Brain CT and MRI: No remarkable findings- Other normal tests included:  - Complete blood count  - Liver/kidney function  - Thyroid function    - Infection markers  - Blood glucose/HbA1c  - Hormone levels  - Autoimmune markers  - EEG/ECG  - Ultrasounds of thyroid, heart, liver and other organs",
